# Taxonomy × Backend Integration: Qualitative Prompt Approach

## 0. Design Philosophy

**Core principle**: Taxonomy findings are injected as **natural-language knowledge** into LLM prompts,
NOT as probability distributions or numerical weights.

**Why**: 
- Avoids reviewer challenges on "where do these probabilities come from"
- Leverages LLM's reasoning ability to interpret qualitative patterns
- More robust: LLM can handle edge cases that rigid probability rules cannot
- Taxonomy findings serve as "empirical grounding" for prompt design, not statistical parameters

**How it works**:
```
Taxonomy Findings (F1-F6)
       ↓
Qualitative descriptions embedded in prompts
       ↓
LLM reasons about role, operations, shift based on these descriptions
       ↓
RuleGuard validates output against hard constraints only
```

---

## 1. Taxonomy Findings Summary (for prompt embedding)

### F1: Bridger Gateway Effect
> In real-world propagation, ~80% of framing shift chains originate from 
> "objective data publishers" (government agencies, research institutions). 
> These Bridgers provide the raw material that all subsequent actors modify.

### F2: Asymmetric Role Transitions
> - Bridger → Amplifier is the dominant transition (most common path)
> - Bridger → Extensioner is the second most common
> - Adverser is a terminal role: once a node adopts an adversarial stance, 
>   the propagation chain tends to end or be forwarded without further modification
> - Amplifier can chain to Amplifier, creating cumulative minor shifts

### F3: Role-Operation Signatures
> - Amplifiers primarily use visual and text-level operations (color changes, 
>   annotations, title modifications) — "change the packaging, not the data"
> - Extensioners primarily use data-level operations (adding variables, 
>   extending time ranges) — "broaden the scope"
> - Adversers use selective data operations (cherry-picking ranges, 
>   removing sources) — "reshape the evidence base"
> - Bridgers rarely modify; when they do, it's minor visual adjustments

### F4: Cross-Platform Escalation
> When a visualization moves from a high-authority platform (government, 
> research institution) to a low-authority platform (social media, personal blog), 
> the framing shift tends to be larger. Same-platform transfers tend to produce 
> smaller, incremental shifts.

### F5: Adverser Threshold
> Major (extreme) framing shifts only occur when an Adverser is involved. 
> Without adversarial reframing, shifts remain at minor-to-significant levels.

### F6: Operation Complexity Correlates with Shift Magnitude
> Single-layer operations (e.g., only visual changes) produce minor shifts.
> Cross-layer combinations (data + visual + text simultaneously) almost always 
> produce significant or major shifts.

